Over my house you flew. I heard the low hum of your blades well before I saw
you. That's how sound travels. I saw you in the deep distance. You were
beautifully black against the Carolina blue sky.

A low rumble. You sounded like my neighbor's Mustang coasting down the street,
out on another adventure. Brrr. But your sound was different. An unyielding
drone. Brrr. Nothing broke in your sound to permit the shift a Mustang would
need. There was no need to change gears. Your blades knew nothing of that.

When you flew over my head the first time I did not know what you were. I was
unfamiliar with your airframe. I simply saw you. I simply witnessed you.
Silhouette. Power. Many of you flew overhead that day. Helicopters with blades
that spun fast and buzzed low and many in a formation. Low. Loud. Cacophonous. I
will never forget that day. I will never forget that sound.

<figure>
[[!template id="hooya-img.tmpl" cid="bafkreigpbfogwio6sh2vnsuzer5xwxn6cm2yzdnplnzzgm6tqkulxiruy4" size=medium]]
</figure>

Today, I saw you again. I understand what you are. You are the Apache AH-64E.
1-130th Attack Battalion. North Carolina National Guard.

You are weapons of war. You are men and women with missions. Pilots with vectors
home and gunners with radios tuned for the airports at which you train. On clear
days your flight path goes over my home.

When I hear you approach from far away, blades in the deep, I never fail to run
outside and witness you fly by. No matter what I am doing in the interim. I
frantically wave at you from the ground. I do not care that you see me. I do not
know you. But I love to wave at you.

No matter how mundane your mission is on any given day, to me, your mission is
the highlight of my day. Your vector is over my home most days. I watch you fly
over me.

You are up there. I am down here.

<figure>
[[!template id="hooya-img.tmpl" cid="bafkreifznfgrggdjuejzyogs7wjqexr6bx7vbp57gcd3p4apz2ihkfogda" size=medium]]
</figure>

Often when I hear your rotors rumble in the deep distnace I am trapped in
meetings or I am making decisions. When I hear you, when I hear the dirty rumble
of your props underneath all else I drop everything. I make an excuse to turn my
camera off. I vacate my obligations. Nothing is so important.

I run. I sprint outside. I do not give a single fuck. Few things are as
important to me as witnessing you when you crest my home. I love to wave at you.
I do not care that you see me. I do not know you. You are up there and I am down
here. I love to wave at you; it is important to me.

You fly over me.

I watch you slip into the blue distance. Going, going, gone. As I watch you
leave, a single feeling dominates me:

I want to be you. I want to be up there. I want to fly the machine. I want to
pilot the machine. I want to fly free shallow along the bends of the Neuse
River. I want to fly over the bump of the dam. I want to fly low beside the
interstate where men in cars drive. And wave at the men on the road.

Men may dream. I dream. God knows, I dream.
